+# Feature Specification: JSON Patch
+
+**Standard**: RFC 6902 (JSON Patch), sections 3 (document structure), 4 (operations: add 4.1, remove 4.2,
+replace 4.3, move 4.4, copy 4.5, test 4.6), 5 (error handling). Paths use RFC 6901 (JSON Pointer) syntax.
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+### Story 1 - Apply a patch (P1)
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies the operations in order and returns a new document; inputs are never mutated.
+- add: object member added or replaced; array element inserted before index (`-` appends; index == length allowed); `""` replaces the whole document.
+- remove / replace: target must exist. move: remove from `from`, add at `path`. copy: deep copy of `from` added at `path`. test: deep equality.
+### Story 2 - Reject invalid patches and failed operations (P1)
+Throws `JsonPatchError` when: patch is not an array; an operation is not an object, has a missing/non-string `op`,
+`path` or `from` (move/copy), lacks `value` (add/replace/test), or has an unknown `op`; a target is missing, an
+array index is invalid or out of range, the parent of an add target does not exist; `move` `from` is a proper
+prefix of `path` (section 4.4); a `test` fails (section 4.6). Invalid pointers throw `JsonPatchError` too.
+
+## Requirements
+- FR-1 Operations apply sequentially; an error aborts the whole patch (section 5) and the input is unchanged.
+- FR-2 `test` compares per 4.6: same type; objects equal if same members and equal values (order ignored); arrays element-wise in order; numbers by value; `null` distinct from missing.
+- FR-3 Inputs not mutated, and result shares no structure with the patch values or input (values are deep-copied).
+- FR-4 `__proto__`, `constructor`, `prototype` keys are plain data keys; they never touch prototypes.
+- FR-5 Members other than those defined for an operation are ignored (section 4).
+
+## Assumptions
+- Public API addition (minor). README updated. `move` with `from` equal to `path` is a no-op once `from` exists.
